# No Redundant Tests

Do not add a new test that only repeats assertions already covered by another test in the same suite.

## Rules

1. **Reuse default-value checks.** If a test like "should get X with default values" (or similar) already asserts default/empty values for a field, do not add a separate test that only checks that same field's default. Add the assertion to the existing test instead.

2. **Prefer one test per behavior.** When adding a new behavior (e.g. a new optional field), either:
   - Extend an existing test that already builds the same object (e.g. "default values" test) with the new default assertion, or
   - Add a single test that covers the new behavior when the value is provided (e.g. "should include X when provided").

3. **Avoid duplicate scenarios.** Before adding a test, scan the describe block for a test that already runs the same scenario (same setup, same call, same kind of outcome). If found, extend that test instead of adding a new one.

## Examples

```javascript
// ❌ BAD: Redundant test — default already asserted in "should get an order with default values"
it("should default paid to false and receipt to null when not provided", () => {
  const order = createOrder();
  expect(order.paid).to.be.false;
  expect(order.receipt).to.be.null;
});

// ✅ GOOD: Defaults covered in the existing default-values test (e.g. expect(order.paid).to.be.false; expect(order.receipt).to.be.null)
// Only add a test for the "when provided" case:
it("should include paid and receipt when provided", () => {
  const order = createOrder({ paid: true, receipt: { id: "x" } });
  expect(order.paid).to.be.true;
  expect(order.receipt).to.deep.equal({ id: "x" });
});
```
